# 0002. One task owns the state; everything else sends events

- Status: Proposed
- Date: 2026-11-24

Several agents stream at once while the user types. This ADR fixes how concurrent input
reaches the app state without shared locks.

## Technical details
- One app task owns `App` (all state: threads, participants, input, scroll, limits). No
  `Arc<Mutex<App>>` anywhere.
- Every source sends an `Event` into one `tokio::sync::mpsc` channel:
  - keyboard and resize events (a crossterm `EventStream` task),
  - participant output (one task per running participant, see
    [0004](0004-cli-agent-adapters.adr) and [0005](0005-openai-compatible-models.adr)),
  - router decisions ([0007](0007-model-to-model-communication.adr)),
  - a tick (~30 fps cap) for spinners and redraw throttling.
- The loop: `recv event → app.update(event) → effects`; `update` is a plain function
  (state in, state + list of `Effect`s out), so it is unit-testable without a terminal.
- `Effect`s are executed by the loop: spawn or cancel a participant task, append to the
  event log ([0003](0003-sqlite-event-log.adr)), write config. Their results come back as
  new `Event`s.
- Redraw only when state changed and at most once per tick.
- Cancellation: each participant task holds a `CancellationToken`; disabling a model or
  `Esc` on a running reply cancels it. The reply is stored as `interrupted` only after
  the adapter confirms its subprocess and tools are gone
  ([0004](0004-cli-agent-adapters.adr)), never on the token alone.
- Backpressure: the channel is bounded (e.g. 1024); participant tasks coalesce text deltas
  when it is full rather than block the subprocess pipe.

Consequences: no lock ordering bugs, deterministic tests of `update`, one place to read to
understand behaviour. Cost: every new capability needs an `Event`/`Effect` variant.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: tokio mpsc, tokio-util `CancellationToken`, crossterm `EventStream`
- Alternatives: shared `Arc<Mutex<State>>` across tasks: simpler at first, but lock contention and redraw races with several streams; an actor framework (actix, ractor): more machinery than one loop needs
